course: dictionary
lesson: 13805
title: Words with several meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Involvement is a noun, a word for a thing or idea. It means taking part in something, or being connected to it. The root word is involve, and the ending -ment turns it into a noun. Some words have more than one meaning, so context helps you choose the right one.
explain: «Her involvement in the school play was great: she painted the scenery and helped with the costumes.» The clues are painted, scenery and costumes. Involvement means taking part. Check by swapping in your meaning: "Her taking part in the school play was great" still makes sense.
explain: The same word can mean being connected to something wrong. «The police asked about his involvement in the robbery.» The word robbery is the clue. The police are checking whether he took part in a crime, so here involvement means being linked to something bad.
explain: Teachers also use the word. In a school report, «good involvement in class discussions» means the child joins in and speaks up. Involvement can also mean strong interest or feeling for something. Read the words around it to find which meaning the writer wants.
fact: Involvement is a noun, and its plural is involvements.
fact: «Involvement» means taking part in something, such as «your involvement in the school play».
fact: «Involvement» can mean being connected to something, often something wrong, such as «his involvement in the robbery».
fact: «Involvement» can also mean strong interest or feeling for something, or a close relationship with someone.
fact: The ending -ment often turns a verb into a noun, as in involve becoming involvement.
exercise: «Her involvement in the school play was great: she painted the scenery.» What does involvement mean here? | A short, rude reply | Being sad about something | Being very old | *Taking part in something
exercise: «The police asked about his involvement in the robbery.» What does involvement suggest? | Feeling very hungry | Winning a prize | *Being connected to something wrong | Standing still
exercise: Mrs Lee writes in a report, «good involvement in class discussions». What does this praise mean? | The child is very old | *The child joins in and speaks up | The child was absent | The child is always late
test: «Parent involvement in the school helps everyone.» Which meaning fits best? | *Parents taking part in school life | Parents being very hungry | Parents who are short and rude | Parents who are very old
test: «Sam's strong involvement in music shows how much he cares about it.» What does involvement mean here? | Being lost in a forest | *Strong interest or feeling for something | A quiet whisper | Cold weather
exam: Which sentence uses involvement to mean being connected to something wrong? | Her involvement in the choir made her happy. | *«The judge asked about his involvement in the theft.» | The sea looked calm and blue.
